--- body ---
## Context
Surfaced by /verify on PR #269 (raid 219-224, ticket 0220): the PR
changed helpMigrate but the committed docs/erg-manual.md (a tracked
generated handoff artifact) was stale -- the execute agent ran
`make docs` but forgot to commit the output. CI did not catch it:
tests/test_docs.sh only checks the manual is non-empty, never that
it is current. The verify gate caught it empirically (REROLL round 1)
and committed the regen; without that gate the stale manual would
have merged.

## Actions
1. Add a drift check to tests/test_docs.sh: regenerate the manual
   (make docs or the underlying command) into a temp file and diff
   against the committed docs/erg-manual.md; fail on any difference.

## Test
With a deliberately edited (stale) docs/erg-manual.md, the suite
fails; after `make docs`, it passes.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] test_docs.sh fails when docs/erg-manual.md is stale against
      the binary's help text
- [ ] Suite remains offline (no network) and hermetic
- [ ] make check passes

## Imagine (raid 2026-06-04)
- Why now: PR #269 slipped a stale docs/erg-manual.md through CI -- the only
  test was non-empty, so a help-text change with no manual regen was invisible
  until /verify caught it empirically. The false-safety is demonstrated and
  the cost is paid once per ticket; this closes the gap before the next wave.
- Scope check: hermetic -- `erg --help --all` makes no network calls and has
  no discovery (ERG_VERSION_NO_DISCOVER is only needed by `erg version`). The
  `docs: build` Makefile chain rebuilds `build/erg` from current source before
  generating, so the guard compares the committed manual against output from
  the PR's own source -- not the stale bootstrap binary. No byte-diff
  normalization is needed: `--help --all` embeds no build date or revision
  (buildDate/vcsRevision appear only in `erg version` output, not help text;
  the header line "Generated from: erg" is static). Maintenance note: if a
  future change routes date/revision into help text, a normalization filter
  will be needed before the diff step.
- Simplest path: test_docs.sh already runs `make docs` (line 39) to check
  non-emptiness -- that call already clobbers the working-tree copy. The
  drift guard is nearly free: capture the regen output into a temp file (or
  use `git diff --exit-code docs/erg-manual.md` after the make docs call) and
  fail on any difference. The self-coherence guard (Makefile comment L88) and
  the CI rebuild job both use `git diff --exit-code` on generated artifacts --
  established repo precedent for exactly this shape.
- Antipattern scan: not tautological -- `_test-lint` hard-forbids tests
  defaulting to the committed `tickets/erg`; the regeneration uses
  `build/erg` rebuilt fresh from source, so the comparison is PR source output
  vs committed manual. Not YAGNI -- the failure mode is documented and already
  cost one REROLL cycle. No premature abstraction -- one diff call in an
  existing test function, no new helpers needed.

## Plan (raid 2026-06-04)
- Actions:
  1. tests/test_docs.sh, after line 44 (end of the non-empty block), add a new
     check using the same pass()/fail() idiom (lines 9-10):
       ```
       if git diff --exit-code docs/erg-manual.md >/dev/null 2>&1; then
           pass "make docs: docs/erg-manual.md is current (no drift)"
       else
           fail "make docs: docs/erg-manual.md is stale -- run 'make docs' and commit"
       fi
       ```
     Exact insertion point: after line 44 (`fi`) and before the blank line at
     line 46 (the README validate test). The `make docs` call at line 39 already
     overwrites docs/erg-manual.md; this check fires on the result.
  2. No other files need changing. The Makefile docs target (lines 138-140) and
     the test harness wiring (Makefile line 55: `sh tests/test_$*.sh` from repo
     root) are already correct.

- First test (prove it works + prove it can fail):
  PASS path -- run `make test-docs` on a clean checkout (docs current):
    expects "PASS: make docs: docs/erg-manual.md is current (no drift)"
  FAIL path (negative control, one-off manual test documented in PR body):
    edit docs/erg-manual.md to add a stale line, then run `make test-docs`;
    expects "FAIL: make docs: docs/erg-manual.md is stale -- run 'make docs'"
  The negative control is a one-off manual step in the PR body -- not a
  permanent in-suite negative control -- because: (a) `make docs` on line 39
  always overwrites the file before the check, so any stale state injected
  earlier in the test run is already gone; (b) injecting drift mid-test (after
  `make docs`) then cleaning it up would leave the tree dirty on test exit;
  (c) selfcoherence.sh's tmpdir pattern shows the approved idiom for permanent
  negative controls, but here a one-off PR-body demonstration is sufficient to
  validate the guard without complicating the test.

- Dependencies:
  - `docs/erg-manual.md` must be a tracked git file (confirmed: it is committed
    as a handoff artifact per git.md).
  - Tests run from repo root (Makefile line 55: `sh tests/test_$*.sh`), so
    `git diff --exit-code docs/erg-manual.md` resolves relative to repo root.
  - The `git` binary must be present in CI (confirmed: CI builds from source,
    git is always present).
  - Hermeticity: `git diff --exit-code` is local-only, no network calls.
  - `ERG_VERSION_NO_DISCOVER` is not needed: `--help --all` does not call
    `erg version` and embeds no build date/revision in help text (buildDate
    and vcsRevision appear only in `erg version` output, confirmed in
    Makefile ldflags lines 34 and src/go context from Imagine section).

- Tautology scan:
  NOT tautological. The comparison is `build/erg --help --all` (freshly compiled
  from PR source by `make docs` -> `make build` prerequisite) vs the committed
  `docs/erg-manual.md`. A developer who changes help text in Go source but
  omits `make docs` before committing will have a stale committed manual; the
  fresh build produces different output; `git diff --exit-code` exits non-zero.
  The guard catches a wrong implementation (stale committed manual) not just a
  different one. The `_test-lint` guard (Makefile lines 43-47) independently
  enforces that ERG defaults to `build/erg`, not `tickets/erg`, ruling out the
  bootstrap-binary shortcut that would make the test tautological.
